<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    P4 calls / prototype-model registry (`cspecprotos`).

    kuna's `__thiscall` machinery was fully ported and permanently dead:
    ProtoModel::hasThisPointer, FuncProto::hasThisPointer/updateThisPointer/
    getThisPointerStorage and ParameterPieces::isThisPointer all exist, are
    driven from ActionInputPrototype and are consumed by P5 type inference,
    but NOTHING could ever set hasThis, because the cspec reader
    (Architecture::build_default_proto) parsed only the ONE model inside
    <default_proto>.  The prototype-model registry held exactly one entry, so
    `getModel("__thiscall")` never resolved and `option defaultprototype
    __thiscall` failed with "Unknown prototype model :__thiscall", even though
    x86win.cspec has DEFINED __thiscall all along.

    This testcase registers the spec's NAMED models (<prototype>,
    <resolveprototype>, <modelalias>) so more than one calling convention can
    live in the registry at once.  Registration alone selects nothing: the
    default pipeline is byte-identical (675/675 datatest assertions unmoved).

    The bytes are two x86 member-function bodies as MSVC emits them, with the
    `this` pointer in ECX:

        getfield @ 0x401000   mov eax,[ecx+0xc]        ; return this->f
                              ret
        addfield @ 0x401004   mov eax,[ecx]            ; return this->g + x
                              add eax,[esp+4]
                              ret

    Evaluated with the <default_proto> model alone (__stdcall), ECX is not in
    the model's <input> pentry list, so the `this` pointer is NOT a parameter:
    it is emitted as an uninitialized local read before assignment (`int4 v1;
    // ecx`), which is not valid C and loses the member access.  Asserts
    #1/#2/#3 pin that this never happens: the spec's <eval_current_prototype>
    nomination (kuna_evalcurrentproto.rs, always on) resolves the merged
    __fastcall/__thiscall/__stdcall model per function and recovers ECX.

    Pass 2 (`option defaultprototype __thiscall`, the model this PR registers)
    must run and give the same answer: ECX is __thiscall's first <pentry>, so
    the `this` pointer is a real parameter and the stack argument sits in the
    second slot.  Asserts #4/#5/#6 count both passes.  Before the registry
    pass 2 could not run at all: the option aborted the script.

    Assigning __thiscall to member functions automatically (from the demangler
    or DWARF DW_AT_object_pointer) is the follow-up; this testcase drives the
    model by hand, which is exactly what the registry now makes possible.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x401000">
8b410cc38b0103442404c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401000" name="getfield"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x401004" name="addfield"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <!-- kuna: `evalcurrentproto` default-on (DIV-71) makes the spec's own
       <eval_current_prototype> merged model recover ECX with no model named by
       hand, which is this testcase's pass-2 result reached automatically. Pin
       the historical <default_proto>-only evaluation here so both passes keep
       testing what they were written for: that the NAMED models are in the
       registry and one can be selected. The automatic path is
       tests/stages/kuna-evalcurrentproto.xml. -->
  <com>lo fu getfield</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu addfield</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option defaultprototype __thiscall</com>
  <com>lo fu getfield</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu addfield</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #1: the ECX this-pointer is never dropped from getfield" min="0" max="0">unsigned int getfield\(void\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #2: the this-pointer never degrades to an uninitialized local" min="0" max="0">// ecx</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #3: addfield never takes only its stack argument" min="0" max="0">int4 addfield\(int4 a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #4: the spec's nominated model and the registered __thiscall model both recover ECX as the first parameter" min="2" max="2">unsigned int getfield\(void \*a0\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #5: the member access is a parameter dereference in both passes" min="2" max="2">return \(\(unsigned int \*\)a0\)\[3\];</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="cspecprotos #6: the stack argument sits in the second slot behind the this-pointer in both passes" min="2" max="2">int4 addfield\(int4 \*a0,int4 a1\)</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
